Let one elastic PodSet span flavors through split PodSets of the same Workload.

A Workload PodSet has one flavor per resource, and a scale-up slice keeps each
PodSet on the flavor of the slice it replaces while it has pods. An elastic
worker group whose growth does not fit on that flavor stays pending even when
the next flavor in the ClusterQueue has room, and a second Workload for the
overflow would break the one-Workload-per-job rule of KEP-77.

With ElasticJobsViaWorkloadSlicesFlavorSplit on, the flavor assigner notes in a
refused replacement slice's message when a PodSet's growth would fit on a flavor
that the sticky-flavor check skipped. Once the scheduler has refused a pending
scale-up probe with that note, the job reconciler keeps each PodSet's
newest entry at what the replaced slice runs and moves the growth to a split
entry, <podSet>--<n>, marked with the template annotation
kueue.x-k8s.io/flavor-split-of. The new entry is absent from the replaced slice,
so the flavor assigner gives it the first flavor that fits. Where Kueue matches
job PodSets with slice PodSets, the job's counts are mapped onto the entries:
growth goes to the newest entry, a shrink of an admitted slice follows the pods
the job's controller deleted, and newest entries shrink first among pods not
started yet. New slices carry the entries and drop those at 0. The
ElasticJobUngater starts each gated pod in an entry with room, writing the entry
in the pod's kueue.x-k8s.io/podset label with the entry's flavor placement.
Split entries get floor 0 and their PodSet's step, so partial preemption of one
flavor stops only that flavor's pods. The job gets the PodSet info of its own
PodSets only. Everything but the split itself follows the entries, not the gate.

